subcode:: sections
    $call section, perl, $(code:output_perl)
    $p
        $(code:output_perl) source is kept within the base $(code:MyDef) repository because, well, $(code:MyDef) is coded in perl and would require $(code:output_perl) to compile. 
    $p
        $(code:output_perl) is also one of the most mature modules, due to mandatory round-trip testing. In addition, because Perl is a very high-level language and embraces practical expressiveness, $(code:output_perl) is relatively simple.
    $p
        Perl is a general purpose programming language, therefore provides the usual $(code:variable), $(code:functions), $(code:scopes), $(code:if-else) switch, $(code:loops), etc. The custom syntax created with $(code:output_perl) can be a good example for output modules for other languages.

    $call subsection, perl_plain, It is OK to write vanilla Perl
    &call codeprint, mydef
        page: t
            my $a = 0.1;
            my $b = 0.2;
            if ($a+$b == 0.3){
                print "$a + $b = 0.3!\n";
            }
            else{
                print "Total failure!\n";
            }
    &call codeprint, sh
        $ mydef_run -mperl t.def
        PAGE: t
          --> [./t.pl]
        perl ./t.pl
        Total failure!

    $p
        And you can check the output $(code:t.pl) is (almost) exactly as you wrote:
    &call codeprint, perl
        &num;!/usr/bin/perl
        use strict;
        my $a = 0.1;
        my $b = 0.2;
        if ($a+$b == 0.3){
            print "$a + $b = 0.3!\n";
        }
        else{
            print "Total failure!\n";
        }
    $p
        It only adds the $(code:#!) line and $(code:use strict;). It is recommended that $(code:strict) should always be used. However, if you disagree, you could set the $(code:relax) option:
    &call codeprint, mydef
        page: t
            module: perl
            relax: 1
            ...
    $p
        and $(code:t.pl) then starts with just the $(code:&num;!) line.

    $call subsection, perl_print, $(code:$print)
    $p
        $(code:print) is the most fundamental and useful statement in a language -- not because it is essential in the final program, but because it is essential for providing feedback during code development. Programming is a process.
    $p
        Perl is one of the most straight forward languages. The "Hello world" program that listed in the first chapter of "Programming Perl" is:
    &call codeprint, perl
        print "Howdy, world!\n";
    $p
        To most programmers, this is as basic as it can be. But nevertheless, over years of experience, a few wishes do pop up: Is it necessary to type those quotation marks, and '$(code:\n)', and the semicolons? The annoyance pops up because we do forget or mistype them from time to time and then have the compiler bug us with errors.
    $p
        Turns out, in most cases, no, it isn't necessary. So in $(code:output_perl), we introduced a hack:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            $print Howdy, world!
    $p
        In modern versions of Perl, there is a new function $(code:say), which essentially is $(code:print_ln) (print with newline). $(code:$print) achieves the same without it.
    $p
        There is more to it. What is the output of this:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            $print Howdy, world!\n
    $p
        You can quickly check it yourself, but the answer is, no, it won't print two newlines. There is a little intelligence in $(code:output_perl) that only adds newline when newline is missing. By the way, both of the following works as well:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            $print "Howdy, world!"
            $print "Howdy, world!\n"
    $p
        A little flexibility goes a long way.
    $p
        Now what if we want to print without newline?
        &call codeprint, mydef
            $print Howdy, -
            $print world
    $p
        '$(code:-)' in the end signals that we do not want newline. We usually want to print with newline, but occasionally we don't. So the philosophy here is to make the default for common scenarios, and go extra for special cases.
    $p
        Mixing variables into the string still works. In addition, there is a little hack to make print in color easier:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            my $name = "world";
            $print Howdy, $green{$name}!
    $p
        Supported color names include: $(code:red), $(code:green), $(code:yellow), $(code:blue), $(code:magenta), $(code:cyan). You will have trouble if you have a variable named with one of these color names and want to print it. Hopefully that is rare, but if you do, remember that vanilla Perl's $(code:print) is still there.
    $p
        Perl's variable interpolation in strings is great. But sometimes we still want to print with a format. For that, we would need to remember another function: $(code:printf). $(code:$print) supports that as well:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            $print "Pi = %.2f", 3.1415926
    $p
        It is equivalent to:
        &call codeprint, perl
            printf "Pi = %.2f\n", 3.1415926;

    $p
        One more thing: sometimes we want to print to a filehandle, such as $(code:STDERR). We can still use $(code:$print) for that. All we need is to set a special macro:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            $(set:print_to=STDERR)
            $print Error: did you just want an error?

    $p
        Put together, the forms above:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            my $name = "world";
            $print Howdy, world!
            $print Howdy, world!\n
            $print "Howdy, world!"
            $print Howdy, -
            $print world
            $print Howdy, $green{$name}!
            $print "Pi = %.2f", 3.1415926
            $(set:print_to=STDERR)
            $print Error: did you just want an error?
    $p
        become:
        &call codeprint, perl
            my $name = "world";
            print "Howdy, world!\n";
            print "Howdy, world!\n";
            print "Howdy, world!\n";
            print "Howdy, ";
            print "world\n";
            print "Howdy, \x1b[32m$name\x1b[0m!\n";
            printf "Pi = %.2f\n", 3.1415926;
            print STDERR "Error: did you just want an error?\n";

    $p
        That's it for $(code:$print)! If you want further detail, directly consult the source in $(code:output_perl.def) and search for $(code:subcode: parsecode_print). The implementation for all this flexibility is not complicated, thanks to Perl's built-in convenience.

    $call subsection, perl_semi, Optional semicolons
    $p
        In the previous section, we saw that $(code:print) not only lets you omit typing quotation marks and '$(code:\n)', you also get to omit semicolons. In fact, with $(code:output_perl), you can optionally omit semicolons for almost every normal Perl statement. Try this:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            my $name = "Alice"
            if ($name eq "Alice"){
                $print Howdy, $name
            }
            else{
                $print Nice to meet you, $name!
            }
        &call codeprint, perl
            my $name = "Alice";
            if ($name eq "Alice"){
                print "Howdy, $name\n";
            }
            else{
                print "Nice to meet you, $name!\n";
            }
    $p
        Surely, $(code:MyDef) did not add '$(code:;)' to every line. It employs some simple heuristics. In fact, you can just check what it does in the source:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            subcode: check_termination
                $if $l=~/^\s*$/
                    &num; NOOP
                $elif $l=~/^\s*(for|while|if|else if)\s*\(.*\)\s*$/
                    &num; NOOP
                $elif $l=~/^\s*}/
                    &num; Check the formal block end
                    &num; Inline blocks will get ; appended.
                $elif $l!~/[,:\(\[\{;]\s*$/
                    $l.=";";
                $else
                    &num; NOOP

    $call subsection, perl_if, $(code:$if), $(code:$elif), and $(code:$else)
    $p
        These are syntactic sugar for $(code:if-elsif-else) statements. They allow us to write control statements in the style of Python rather than C's curly-brace style. Here is an example:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            for(my $i=1; $i<100; $i++){
                $if $i % 15 == 0
                    $print fizbuzz
                $elif $i % 3 == 0
                    $print fiz
                $elif $i % 5 == 0
                    $print buzz
                $else
                    $print $i
            }
        &call codeprint, perl
            for(my $i=1; $i<100; $i++){
                if ($i % 15 == 0) {
                    print "fizbuzz\n";
                }
                elsif ($i % 3 == 0) {
                    print "fiz\n";
                }
                elsif ($i % 5 == 0) {
                    print "buzz\n";
                }
                else {
                    print "$i\n";
                }
            }
    $p
        Obviously there is a similar indentation-based syntax for $(code:for)-loops, but since that is in the next section, we show the example above to demonstrate that there is nothing wrong with mixing vanilla Perl code with the special syntax introduced by $(code:output_perl).
    $p
        It is common to use $(code:if-elif-else) as poor man's $(code:switch). And for big switch block, it is often desirable to break it apart:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            foreach my $l (@lines){
                $if $l=~/^\s*$/
                    &num; NOOP
                $call @l_is_special
                $else
                    $print "nothing special"
            }

            subcode:: l_is_special
                $elif $l=~/^special: .*/
                    $print SPECIAL!
    $p
        That works. But then there is this nagging thing that you always need differentiate the first case, where you should use $(code:$if), and the other cases, where you should use $(code:$elif). This makes swapping the orders of cases buggy. For remedy, $(code:output_perl) has another keyword: $(code:$case):
        &call codeprint, mydef
            foreach my $l (@lines){
                $call @check_cases
                $else
                    $print "nothing special"
            }

            subcode:: check_cases
                $case $l=~/^\s*$/
                    &num; NOOP

            subcode:: check_cases
                $case $l=~/^special: .*/
                    $print SPECIAL!
    $p
        $(code:$case) becomes $(code:$if) if it is the first case, and becomes $(code:$elif) for subsequent cases. For example, the $(code:check_cases) subcode above generates:
        &call codeprint, perl
            if ($l=~/^\s*$/){
            }
            elsif ($l=~/^special: .*/){
                print "SPECIAL!\n";
            }
    $p
        The first $(code:$case) emitted $(code:if), and the second emitted $(code:elsif). This lets you freely reorder or add cases without worrying about which one must be $(code:$if). Due to its heuristic nature, $(code:$case) is a little more fragile than $(code:$if) and $(code:$elif).

    $p
        One of the top bugs is mistyping $(code:==) as $(code:=) in $(code:if) conditions. So $(code:output_perl) checks for that when it can and gives you warnings:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            my $i=1
            $if $i = 15
                $print fizbuzz

        &call codeprint, sh
            $ mydef_run t.def
            PAGE: t
            [t.def:6] assignment in condition [$i = 15]?
              --> [./t.pl]
            perl ./t.pl
            fizbuzz
    $p
        It is only a warning, but if you paid attention, you caught the bug!

    $call subsection, perl_for, $(code:$for)
    $p
        First, let's review the vanilla Perl syntax:
        &call codeprint, perl
            for(my $i=0; $i<10; $i++){
                $print $i
            }
    $p
        $(code:MyDef) works purely at the text level; it does not truly understand the language semantics as a compiler does. It only understands the text via heuristics -- which is more similar to how the human mind works. As such, $(code:MyDef) special syntax does not always work. We start with the vanilla Perl syntax example to emphasize that you can always fall back on the standard syntax if it becomes more trouble than help.
    $p
        That said, let's see what $(code:output_perl) offers:
    &call codeprint, mydef
        $for my $i=0; $i<10; $i++
            $print $i
    $p
        Just like the $(code:$if) syntax, we now can write it in Python style and save the braces. Further, a range can be written with colons -- start&#44; end&#44; and step -- and $(code:output_perl) supplies the $(code:my):
    &call codeprint, mydef
        $for $i=0:10:1
            $print $i
    $p
        The increment by 1 is the default:
    &call codeprint, mydef
        $for $i=0:10
            $print $i
    $p
        In fact, the loop variable $(code:$i) is also the default, so we can drop that too:
    &call codeprint, mydef
        $for 0:10
            $print $i
    $p
        Or:
    &call codeprint, mydef
        $for 10
            $print $i
    $p
        All of these generate the same loop. We did not change Perl, only the syntax:
    &call codeprint, perl
        for (my $i = 0; $i<10; $i++) {
            print "$i\n";
        }
    $p
        Just to make sure, the step part works:
    &call codeprint, mydef
        $for $i=0:10:2
            $print $i

    $p
        The decreasing $(code:for)-loop works too, but it is a bit tricky. It is tricky because it appears there is not a established convention. For example, what do we think the following does?
    &call codeprint, mydef
        $for $i=10:0:-1
            $print $i
    &call codeprint, perl
        for (my $i = 10; $i>=0; $i--) {
            print "$i\n";
        }
    $p
        The current implementation in $(code:output_perl) prints $(code:10), $(code:9), ..., $(code:0). Yep, it loops 11 times instead of 10. We find it easier to reason by taking the literal meaning of "from $(code:10) down to $(code:0)." Many may disagree. As said, tricky. But remember, for clarity you can and maybe should use the more verbose version:

    &call codeprint, mydef
        $for my $i=10; $i>0; $i--
            $print $i
    $p
        This form is passed through as it is&#44; so it needs the $(code:my) under $(code:use strict).

    $call subsection, perl_while, $(code:$while)
    $p
        $(code:$while) exist so we could write $(code:while)-loop in Python style too.
    &call codeprint, mydef
        $while 1
            $print MyDef FOREVER!
            last &num; maybe not
    &call codeprint, perl
        while(1){
            print "MyDef FOREVER!\n";
            last;
        }

    $call subsection, perl_capture, Regex capture with $(code:->)
    $p
        When using $(code:$if) or $(code:$while) with a regex match, you can capture match groups directly into variables using the $(code:->) syntax:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            $if $line=~/^(\w+):\s*(.*)/ -> $key, $value
                $print key=$key, value=$value
    $p
        This generates:
        &call codeprint, perl
            if ($line=~/^(\w+):\s*(.*)/){
                my ($key, $value) = ($1, $2);
                print "key=$key, value=$value\n";
            }
    $p
        Without the $(code:->) syntax, you would need to manually assign $(code:$1), $(code:$2), etc. inside the block. The capture syntax works with $(code:$while) as well:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            $while $text=~/(\w+)/g -> $word
                $print found: $word
        &call codeprint, perl
            while ($text=~/(\w+)/g) {
                my ($word) = ($1);
                print "found: $word\n";
            }
    $p
        Each capture variable is declared with $(code:my), so it is scoped to the block.

    $call subsection, perl_foreach, $(code:$foreach)
    $p
        $(code:foreach) reads better with a keyword $(code:in):
    &call codeprint, mydef
        $foreach $c in split //, "Hello, world!"
            $print $c
    &call codeprint, perl
        foreach my $c (split //, "Hello, world!") {
            print "$c\n";
        }
    $p
        It supplies the $(code:my) keyword and drops parentheses and braces.
    $p
        $(code:$foreach) supports several iteration forms:
    $table ref
        $tr
            $th "Form"
            $th "Example"
            $th "Perl equivalent"
        $tr
            $td "basic"
            $td
                $(code:$foreach $x in @list)
            $td
                $(code:foreach my $x (@list) {)
        $tr
            $td "implicit"
            $td
                $(code:$foreach @list)
            $td
                $(code:foreach (@list) {)
        $tr
            $td "hash"
            $td
                $(code:$foreach $k, $v in %hash)
            $td
                $(code:while (my ($k, $v) = each %hash) {)
        $tr
            $td "indexed"
            $td
                $(code:$foreach $_i, $x in @list)
            $td
                loop with $(code:$_i) counter
        $tr
            $td "zip"
            $td
                $(code:$foreach $_i, $a, $b in @x, @y)
            $td
                parallel iteration using index
    $p
        For the indexed form, the index variable must be one of $(code:$_i), $(code:$_j), or $(code:$_k). The zip form iterates over multiple arrays in parallel using index-based access.

    $call subsection, perl_fn, $(code:fncode) and function arrangement
    $p
        Curly braces are also used in defining Perl functions -- $(code:sub):
    &call codeprint, mydef
        $sub F($x)
            return $x * $x

        $print "3: %d", F(3)
    $p
        Let's check its Perl equivalent:
    &call codeprint, perl
        sub F {
            my ($x)=@_;
            return $x * $x;
        }

        printf "3: %d\n", F(3);
    $p
        You may recall the criticism that Perl does not have function signatures. As far as syntax goes, this lets us define functions with signatures.

    $p
        The $(code:$sub) syntax works, but behaves as a normal statement -- just like $(code:if) or $(code:while). Semantically it is a bit unsatisfactory, as $(code:$sub) merely defines the function rather than having runtime action as other control statements do. Ideally, we would like to have a declarative syntax for functions and move them outside of normal code blocks. In $(code:MyDef), we can use $(code:fncode:)
    &call codeprint, mydef
        page: t
            module: perl
            $print "3: %d", F(3)

        fncode: F($x)
            return $x * $x
    $p
        The function goes after the main code:
    &call codeprint, perl
        printf "3: %d\n", F(3);

        &num; ---- subroutines --------------------------------------------
        sub F {
            my ($x) = @_;
            return $x * $x;
        }
    $p
        If you are writing a $(code:.pm) module, all $(code:fncode:) definitions are included in the output. However, if you are writing a $(code:.pl) script, which is the default, only those functions that are used will be included. $(code:output_perl) detects which functions are being used with very simple heuristics:

    &call codeprint, mydef
        fncode: check_fcall($l)
            $while $l=~/\b(\w+)\(/g
                $call add_function, $1

    $p
        In case the heuristics do not work well and miss a function that you do use, you can always add the function explicitly with $(code:$list):
    &call codeprint, mydef
        page: t
            module: perl
            $list F
            $print "3: %d", F(3)

        fncode: F($x)
            return $x * $x

    $call subsection, perl_global, $(code:$global) and $(code:$use)
    $p
        There is this religion that we should avoid global variables. The practitioners of $(code:MyDef) do not believe in extreme stances. Globals are certainly convenient. If we do use globals, we definitely would like to define them close to where they are relevant rather than pile all of them at the top. Language semantics, on the other hand, do make sense to have all globals piled at the top. To help, $(code:output_perl) provides $(code:$global) to make declaration (and initialization) of global variables declarative.
    &call codeprint, mydef
        page: t
            $print "3: %d", F(3)

        fncode: F($x)
            $global $Offset = 10
            return $x * $x + $Offset
    $p
        We certainly do not want re-declare and reset $(code:$Offset) every time the function is called. The Perl output equivalent is:
    &call codeprint, perl
        #!/usr/bin/perl
        use strict;    
        our $Offset = 10;   

        sub F {                 
            my ($x)=@_;         
            return $x * $x + $Offset
        }                       

        printf "3: %d\n", F(3); 

    $p
        Similarly, $(code:$use) made importing packages declarative so you could embed certain package dependence into relevant code and be confident that they all will be gathered to their rightful place.

    $call subsection, perl_std, $(code:std_perl.def)
    $p
        Finally, not all customization need to happen at the internal level. We can have customization coded in $(code:MyDef) macro syntax and have them included in the $(code:def) file. For each output module, there is one standard library that is always included automatically. For $(code:perl), that is $(code:std_perl.def). It is located at $(code:deflib/) in the repository and at $(code:$MYDEFLIB) in your installed destination.
    $p
        It is easy enough for you to open that file and see for yourself what is in it. It is a collection of convenience macros; some are frequently used while others are rarely used. Those rarely used will probably get pruned away at some point.
    $p
        The most used two subcodes are $(code:subcode: open_r) and $(code:subcode: open_w). $(code:subcode: open_r) allows short code for opening file and read it line by line:
    &call codeprint, mydef
        &call open_r, t.txt
            $if /(\w+), (\w+)/
                $print Hello $2 $1!

    $p
        $(code: subcode: open_w) is similar but obviously it can't write line by line for you:
    &call codeprint, mydef
        &call open_w, t.txt
            $(set:print_to=Out)
            $print Hello, world!
            $print Hello, world, again.
    $p
        $(code:In) is the default input file handle, and $(code:Out) is the default output file handle.
    $p
        If you just want to read in the whole file in a scalar, you can use $(code:subcode: get_file_in_t):
    &call codeprint, mydef
        $call get_file_in_t, t.txt
        $print [$t]
    $p
        There is also $(code:get_file_lines) that slurps entire text file into $(code:@lines) array.
    &call codeprint, mydef
        $call get_file_lines, t.txt
        $print "Got %d lines", $#lines+1
    $p
        Put together:
    &call codeprint, mydef
        page: io
            module: perl
            &call open_w, t.txt
                $(set:print_to=Out)
                $print Alice, Smith
                $print Bob, Jones
            &call open_r, t.txt
                $if /(\w+), (\w+)/
                    $print Hello $2 $1!
            $call get_file_lines, t.txt
            $print "Got %d lines", $#lines+1
    &call codeprint, sh
        $ mydef_run io.def
        PAGE: io
          --> [./io.pl]
        perl ./io.pl
        Hello Smith Alice!
        Hello Jones Bob!
        Got 2 lines
